"""Shared Work validation guidance; enforcement remains in workflow controllers."""
POLICY = "RISK-BASED VALIDATION\nBefore editing, classify the current requested change LOW / MEDIUM / HIGH by behavior, blast radius and reversibility, not file extension alone. Record risk, reasons, affected apps/journeys, baseline commit, focused commands, escalation triggers and remaining gaps in implementation-plan.md (or the existing validation section). State this briefly to the user. Reassess if scope expands. Use the highest material risk in a mixed change. Follow mandatory repository/CI checks and explicit user requirements.\n\nLOW: isolated CSS/markup/template/presentation changes with no business logic, identity, persistence, security or shared contract changes. Frontend TS/Svelte is LOW only under those conditions. A migration scaffold or inactive flag is LOW only if it changes no executable behavior or data. Storage additions/changes are LOW only for isolated, non-sensitive, reversible content with no existing-data or access impact.\nRun focused component tests and the affected browser journey; check narrow phone layout, relevant modes/tenants, keyboard/focus/accessibility and the actual visible result. Use a relevant typecheck when types or templates change. For a pure document change, validate links/content rather than launching an application suite. No automatic Claude review for an ordinary LOW task unless explicitly requested or required by repository policy. Report focused validation, not whole-feature certification.\n\nMEDIUM: bounded SvelteKit/backend/PHP service behavior, additive data changes with compatible defaults, shared UI behavior or state, and changes with a known, contained integration boundary. Run LOW checks as applicable, affected unit/integration suites and direct consumers, success and failure/validation cases, permission boundaries where touched, persistence/reload and backwards compatibility where relevant. Exercise the affected end-to-end journey; include Chromium/WebKit when browser behavior differs or the feature targets iOS. Run affected package typechecks/lint/build as appropriate. Require independent review before claiming ready to merge.\n\nHIGH: data deletion/transformation, identity/schema migrations, authentication/authorization/privacy/payments, destructive storage operations, broad shared contracts, executable feature flags controlling sensitive behavior, Docker/runtime/build/deployment changes, dependencies/lockfiles and CI/hooks/config changes affecting execution or safety. Cosmetic metadata-only edits may be lower risk with an explicit reason. Run comprehensive regression for all affected systems and direct dependents, integration and critical cross-app journeys, relevant supported browsers, builds/static checks, security/negative cases and migration/backfill/idempotence/rollback tests on disposable data as applicable. Use the full repository suite for cross-cutting impact, uncertain blast radius or a release checkpoint; omit unrelated platforms only with recorded justification. Require independent review and explicit human acceptance of material risks before release. Never operate on production data to validate a migration. A review is not permission to merge, push or deploy; HIGH is not automatic permission to close or abandon a PR.\n\nTEST DISCIPLINE\nUse existing project runners and CI commands. For a bug fix, prove a relevant regression test fails before the fix and passes after where feasible; otherwise record the evidence gap. Never weaken assertions, hide skipped tests, replace real behavior with mocks or report unavailable/manual checks as passed. Stop after the selected checks pass: broaden or repeat only for subsequent relevant edits, failures, uncertain impact, explicit instructions or a release checkpoint, and explain why. Reuse recorded evidence only for unchanged relevant source/config/environment. Long runs need a progress update explaining what remains. If runner limits cannot accommodate required checks, report the gap and use the project's existing CI/approved test path; do not silently reduce coverage or raise limits.\n\nSCOPE AND REVIEW\nKeep current-change validation distinct from earlier stream-wide work. Do not automatically rerun every historical acceptance item for a small visual tweak, and do not erase or reclassify earlier blockers. Full-feature readiness still requires all its acceptance criteria. Scope an independent review explicitly to the agreed change and affected regressions; changing existing review scope requires agreement and must retain outstanding broader gaps. Native/device checks remain open until actually performed.\nAn explicit Implement through review or work review request still runs independent review even for LOW risk. The controller owns that review; agents in that flow must not launch it themselves. Normal MEDIUM/HIGH Work tasks use the bounded final review after routine checks pass. Claude remains Sonnet/medium, at most 24 turns/20 minutes per call, no fixed review-call cap; at most four automatic rounds per workflow. Stop on provider limits or incomplete evidence. No extra Claude helpers, paid API fallback, credit purchases or auto-top-up. Do not consume a review merely to rediscover known missing evidence; report that blocker first. A completed review may correctly require changes or remain incomplete. Only the controller's verified verdict is independent approval for its exact recorded version and scope.\n"

POLICY += """
LOCAL COMPLETION GATE
Before selected checks run, create/fill docs/handoffs/<stream>/validation-plan.json (work complete <stream> --init creates a non-passing template). Fields: version 1; risk low/medium/high; scope, rationale and coverage explaining this change, affected behavior and why selected tests suffice; checks listing existing tests-input/review-input check IDs; limitations listing any concrete gaps. High risk also needs risk_controls covering regression scope and recovery/rollback. Keep it brief for a title/style change. Use the project's existing tests, not a new framework. Evidence must match the current checkout; write assessment and test inputs before testing.
For ordinary Work completion, use work tests run <stream> for selected finite checks, then work complete <stream>. Reuse a current passing recorded run instead of rerunning. work complete only checks evidence; it never starts tests or Claude. A low-risk focused pass needs no automatic review. Medium/high risk needs current independent review, or a concrete review_deferred_reason (at least 20 characters) to report completed-with-limitations, never verified/merge-ready. Existing unresolved review findings cannot be bypassed this way. Missing, failed, stale or modified logs never count as passed. Report blockers; do not expand scope, retry indefinitely, weaken tests or reset Claude counters just to obtain a receipt.
Within Implement through review, use the existing work flow ready receipt instead: it checks the risk assessment; the controller then executes the declared checks and review and checks completion before accepting verified. Do not launch work tests or work complete inside that active workflow. Preserve its initial-plus-follow-up Claude limits. Completing a small scoped change does not certify previous unverified work. No completion receipt authorizes merge, push or deploy. This is a local workflow safeguard, not future CI/CD enforcement.
"""

POLICY += """
SMALL VISUAL CHANGES
For copy, spacing, color, icon or layout requests, preserve the existing dialog primitive, focus/keyboard behavior, event handlers, state transitions, permission decisions and persistence. Do not add counters, new business rules or adjacent refactors unless required by the request. If the requested result needs behavioral changes, explain that expansion and reassess before proceeding; do not label it visual-only because it lives in a UI file.
Use change_type visual, behavioral, mixed or documentation in new validation plans. Record behavior_changes as a short list of actual changed interactions/contracts (empty for visual/documentation), and validation_scope focused or broad. Visual changes must be low risk with no behavior_changes; behavioral/mixed changes are at least medium, escalating to high for sensitive behavior. A broad run requires broad_reason explaining affected dependencies, uncertainty, a mandatory repository rule or an explicit release request. These are assessment checks, not automatic semantic proof of the diff.
For a visual change, select existing component assertions and one affected browser journey, combining them in one command where practical. Inspect the actual rendered change and relevant responsive/accessibility states. Include another browser when the touched behavior warrants it or project policy requires it, not every historical browser/app matrix by default. Broad regression is appropriate for cross-cutting/uncertain impact, not simply because a stream has a large historical diff. Keep mandatory repository checks intact.
Before testing, finish the assessment and test inputs. Stop when the selected checks pass. Do not rerun unchanged passing checks for reassurance or unrelated documentation edits; reuse only evidence the current freshness checks accept. Do not alter fingerprints or hide failures to reuse a result. If freshness invalidates evidence, report why and rerun the required configured checks, without expanding their scope.
On failure, inspect the relevant log first. One targeted diagnostic rerun is reasonable for a suspected environmental timeout; record the initial failure and do not claim the rerun proves no flakiness. If it fails again or needs unrelated fixes, report the blocker instead of entering a broad fix/retest cycle. Relevant production fixes still require affected checks again. Keep unrelated outstanding findings visible, never certify them through a small-change receipt. Long-running work must explain the scope expansion or blocker to the user rather than silently chasing a green gate.
"""
